55
00:04:17,820 --> 00:04:20,281
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't just go into nibbāna, because there will be another thing.

56
00:04:23,503 --> 00:04:29,626
[SPEAKER_00]: But on that most general level of experience, the thing...

57
00:04:29,666 --> 00:04:30,267
[SPEAKER_01]: So, exactly.

58
00:04:30,287 --> 00:04:33,288
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for as long as you have the five aggregates, there's always something manifested.

59
00:04:33,929 --> 00:04:34,329
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why.

60
00:04:34,715 --> 00:04:43,162
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, Buddha Vipassi, before he became the Buddha, he was contemplating arising and falling, i.e.

61
00:04:43,242 --> 00:04:47,105
[SPEAKER_01]: manifestations of any kind, of the five aggregates.

62
00:04:47,846 --> 00:04:54,111
[SPEAKER_01]: So, on the most general level, he was contemplating the nature of arising, means nature ceasing, because it has arisen.

63
00:04:55,513 --> 00:04:58,035
[SPEAKER_01]: And he became the Buddha, just through that contemplation.

64
00:05:00,457 --> 00:05:01,798
[SPEAKER_01]: So, exactly, whether you

65
00:05:03,107 --> 00:05:16,930
[SPEAKER_01]: Unless your aggregates break apart, any experience that you witness, that you go through, that you experience, even if it's a destruction of every single thing in the world, even if it's a destruction of your own senses, that arises for you.

66
00:05:18,990 --> 00:05:22,911
[SPEAKER_01]: That disappearance appears for you as disappearance of those things.

67
00:05:24,352 --> 00:05:30,053
[SPEAKER_01]: Because things arise, or rather, everything is discerned and arises on the level of the mind.

68
00:05:30,173 --> 00:05:31,393
[SPEAKER_01]: That's when the mind comes first.

69
00:05:36,994 --> 00:05:39,215
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I have to understand impermanence?

70
00:05:39,596 --> 00:05:49,142
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, in order to understand impermanence, you have to discern the arising of things, their manifestation.

71
00:05:49,162 --> 00:05:53,124
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to discern the nature of their manifestation.

72
00:05:53,685 --> 00:05:54,525
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's it.

73
00:05:54,825 --> 00:06:03,051
[SPEAKER_01]: If you see that they manifest, and you have absolutely... it's inconceivable for you to have any say in their manifestation.

74
00:06:03,291 --> 00:06:05,072
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just like you're completely

75
00:06:06,143 --> 00:06:07,483
[SPEAKER_01]: It's in... it's inaccessible.

76
00:06:07,623 --> 00:06:12,925
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot possibly even conceive it in the utmost obstruction, how it would be.

77
00:06:13,005 --> 00:06:15,006
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just... the mind cannot go there.

78
00:06:15,846 --> 00:06:24,689
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of that, you know, for this very reason, that I have no say in their manifestation, that's why things are impermanent.

79
00:06:25,089 --> 00:06:26,669
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why things are distanced from you.

80
00:06:26,689 --> 00:06:28,950
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why they cannot be myself.

81
00:06:30,911 --> 00:06:31,451
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

82
00:06:32,231 --> 00:06:34,992
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not that you need to figure out, then, what not-self means.

83
00:06:35,486 --> 00:06:40,310
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, by seeing the impermanence correctly for what it is, that's automatically the experience of not-self.

84
00:06:41,691 --> 00:06:42,332
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's the same.

85
00:06:42,812 --> 00:06:44,613
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the same thing.

86
00:06:45,094 --> 00:06:50,278
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's a different thing, but it's basically the same spirit that consists of different things.

87
00:06:51,579 --> 00:06:52,460
[SPEAKER_00]: You're saying it's...

88
00:06:52,480 --> 00:06:55,142
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's... yeah, it's within... it's within the same thing.

89
00:07:04,810 --> 00:07:05,050
[SPEAKER_01]: Because

90
00:07:06,335 --> 00:07:15,901
[SPEAKER_01]: Always, like, when people, when you read the suttas or any contemporary Buddhist, whatever, interpretations, you just assume, oh, impermanence, impermanence.

91
00:07:16,481 --> 00:07:17,862
[SPEAKER_00]: You must see arising.

92
00:07:17,902 --> 00:07:20,063
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, and then you look for the impermanence.

93
00:07:20,603 --> 00:07:23,985
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's why lots of people really commit to it.

94
00:07:24,045 --> 00:07:25,686
[SPEAKER_01]: You end up experiencing frustration.

95
00:07:26,327 --> 00:07:35,492
[SPEAKER_01]: And I know I'm speaking from experience, because no matter how much, like, you know, you might even destroy things that mean to you, just to show.

96
00:07:35,915 --> 00:07:38,896
[SPEAKER_01]: But actually, that's another arising of another experience.

97
00:07:39,936 --> 00:07:42,197
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's the experience that is impermanent.

98
00:07:43,658 --> 00:07:45,678
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what Nicha means, not the actual things.

99
00:07:46,279 --> 00:07:57,902
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, as the... one of the wrong views of the... of the Western science, and I remember we studied it from chemistry, like, nothing can get destroyed, it's only the energy and the molecules which change the shape.

100
00:07:57,962 --> 00:08:00,243
[SPEAKER_01]: So the... the...

101
00:08:00,283 --> 00:08:01,444
[SPEAKER_01]: I forgot the name, so I don't wanna...

102
00:08:01,464 --> 00:08:03,164
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't wanna get it wrong, but...

103
00:08:03,184 --> 00:08:05,365
[SPEAKER_01]: It was the name of a law, inverted commas,

104
00:08:06,203 --> 00:08:20,168
[SPEAKER_01]: Which was basically like when you burn... say you burn a piece of wood, well, that energy, that object, it's just changed its form now.

105
00:08:20,248 --> 00:08:21,329
